"# Final Structure v3.0 — comprehensive\n\n2026-09-26 · @Mohammad AlFalasi\n\n## Version genealogy and audit method\n\nFive documents preceded this one, and v2.1 carried most but not all of them; the audit below checked every unit, principle and recommendation in each against v2.1 and found 38 items missing, weakened or unresolved, none of which required changing the four-level shape, so v3.0 is v2.1 plus those items and a set of structural rules.\n\n| Version | What it is | What it contributed that survives |\n|---|---|---|\n| v0 — the original syllabus | Six tracks, 39 modules, about 250 units, postgraduate register, Gulf-centred | The content at full depth; the Concept DNA method; the twelve grammars; the four boundary tests and blind interpretation; the studios' unit lists; the venture and release realism |\n| The analysis (Instructional Analysis & Build Plan) | Diagnosis of v0; per-module essential questions; 25 interactive patterns; teaching frameworks; resource needs; gaps; build phases | Threads (Planetary Weight, Handover Grammar); the pattern library; threshold concepts; unit anatomy; the five criteria; the gap list; the pipeline phases |\n",
"| Concept v1 | Rebuild for two audiences, global scope, self-paced, no budget | The two lanes; the artifact growing per unit; Level 0; the research plan by module type; the free-resource strategy; the AI partner's first three voices; the Module Factory |\n| Concept v2 | Full depth restored through three depths per module | Depth dials; vocabulary policy; nine angles; named routes; seed worlds; commissioning variants; build order; alternatives considered |\n| Master Structure v2.1 | Unit register: 21 modules, 10 tools modules, 10 engines, about 430 units | The register itself; the artifact schema; tools evolution paths; compound engines; media and lecture studies; research log rules |\n\n",
"**Audit method.** Each earlier document was read unit by unit and recommendation by recommendation; each item was marked present, partial or absent in v2.1; partial and absent items received a fix (a new unit, a renamed unit, a rule, a thread or an appendix). Fixes were then checked for duplication against each other and against the register. The result is §2. Numbering stays stable: a new unit takes the number of the unit it follows plus a letter (F1.3a), so every reference in earlier documents still resolves.\n\n## Gap audit\n\nThirty-eight items; nineteen become new or renamed units, the rest become rules, threads, assets or appendices, and every one is traced to the version it came from.\n\n| # | Element | From | State in v2.1 | Fix in v3.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Interdisciplinary global discourse: what film, performance, sound, gaming and digital culture teach design | v0 1.1 | Absent | New unit F1.3a |\n| 2 | Vernacular intelligence: oral histories, folktales, local taxonomies as sources | v0 1.1 | Only the regional slot | New unit F1.20a, with consent method from M1.5 |\n",
"| 3 | Identity, symbolism, collective memory | v0 1.1 | Partial in F4.7 | F4.7 renamed Semiotics, symbolism and collective memory |\n| 4 | Temporal intelligence from literature, film, sound, performance | v0 2.1 | Partial in M1.11 | New unit M1.11a World-building lessons from film, games and performance |\n| 5 | Practice DNA: the rules of the chosen medium | v0 2.2 | Only implied by the O units | New unit in every area: Sx.1a The practice DNA of this medium |\n| 6 | Omnichannel commerce and spatial retail: checkout, online-to-offline, the flagship as media | v0 5.5 | Absent (dropped in v1) | New units R1.10a, R1.10b, R1.10c; Venture Console carrier layer |\n| 7 | Cost, value and margin architecture | v0 5.9 | Absent (engine layer with no unit) | New unit R2.4a |\n| 8 | Expansion strategy across markets | v0 5.9 | Absent | New unit R2.4b |\n| 9 | Lifecycle economics: total cost of ownership | v0 5.9 | Absent | New unit R4.4a |\n| 10 | Data-rich models: BIM and PLM | v0 4.11 | Only mentioned in S1.31 | New unit T4.2a |\n| 11 | Licensing agreements | v0 6.2 | Absent | R2.6 renamed Contracts, NDAs, licensing, transfer of rights |\n",
"| 12 | Exhibition, museum and cultural-district experience | analysis §11 | Absent | New unit S1.8a |\n| 13 | Large-scale events, expos, activations | analysis §11 | Partial in R1.10 | New unit R1.12a (Mastery case) |\n| 14 | Service design, hospitality, food and drink | analysis §11 | Partial in S1.11 | New unit S1.8b The service script behind the space |\n| 15 | Landscape, desert ecology, water | analysis §11 | Only the regional slot | New units F5.5a Land, water and climate as the site; S1.24a The outdoor room |\n| 16 | Game design and interactive narrative | analysis §11 | Absent | Covered by M1.11a; S6 pattern library references game interfaces |\n| 17 | Scent and culinary design | analysis §11 | S1.21 only | Case dossiers added to S1.21; no new unit |\n| 18 | Jewellery and accessories | analysis §11 | Absent | New unit S3.35a Accessories and jewellery: the object on the body |\n| 19 | Accessibility as a horizontal | analysis §11 | Scattered | Thread 4, §8, with a checkpoint in every studio brief |\n| 20 | Documentation and photography of physical work | analysis §11 | Partial in S4.7, T5.3 | New unit T5.1a |\n",
"| 21 | Client and stakeholder negotiation | analysis §11 | Partial in R3.6 | New unit R3.6a, Client voice |\n| 22 | Writing and argumentation | analysis §11 | Partial in R5.3 | Thread 5, §8: one written argument per level |\n| 23 | Synthetic worlds as a medium | analysis §11 | S4.27–29, T3 | Noted as future area S7 in §11 |\n| 24 | Research plan by module type | v1 §7 | Absent | Carried into §10 |\n| 25 | Unit anatomy and closing tests per module | v1 §9, analysis §9 | Absent | §5 and §6 |\n| 26 | Teaching frameworks and threshold concepts | analysis §9 | Absent | §5 |\n| 27 | The five assessment criteria | analysis §9 | Absent (no grades) | Kept as the partner's five lenses, §9 |\n| 28 | Vocabulary policy | v2 §4 | Absent | §5 |\n| 29 | Nine angles and named routes | v2 §5 | Absent | Routes in §5; angles in §11 |\n| 30 | Seed worlds for learners without a project | v2 §5 | M2.1 only | Asset: three seed worlds, §10 |\n| 31 | Commissioning variant of every studio brief | v2 §5 | Absent | Rule in §5; CMB field in every Sx studio brief |\n| 32 | Pattern library, three long-form patterns, Studio Workspace | analysis §8, v2 §7 | Engines only | Build inventory §11; Studio Workspace added |\n",
"| 33 | Peer overlay when there is no cohort | v1 §9 | Assumed | Rule in §9: pooled anonymised outputs, partner stand-in |\n| 34 | Program voice from approved samples | v1 §10 | Absent | Pipeline §11 |\n| 35 | Learner personas | v1 rhythm | Pending | Next steps §12 |\n| 36 | Commissioned regional assets | analysis §10 | Absent | Program-produced assets list, §10, regional ones deferred |\n| 37 | Institutions, partners, reading canon, kits | analysis §10 | Absent | Appendix in §11: canon for our grounding, partners for later, things-at-hand instead of kits |\n| 38 | Hours by depth and prerequisites | analysis §2, v2 §3 | Absent from the register | §6 |\n\n## Logic and efficiency review\n\nThe four-level shape and the depth dials hold; what v2.1 lacked was the connective tissue — threads, closing tests, prerequisites, reuse marking and a rule for briefers inside the studios — and those are the structural changes adopted here.\n\n| Finding | Problem it caused | Adjustment |\n|---|---|---|\n",
"| Cross-cutting concerns (planetary weight, handover, attribution, accessibility, writing, AI collaboration) appeared as scattered units | A learner meets them six times without progression | Six horizontal threads with named checkpoints per level (§8); the units stay where they are, the thread gives them order |\n| Modules had no closing test | Nothing marks a module as finished for either lane | A closing test per module, both lanes (§6) |\n| No prerequisites in the register | Sequencing was implied, not stated | A prerequisite column (§6) and four sequencing rules (§5) |\n| Labs reused across modules were indistinguishable from new ones | Build effort could not be counted | Reuse marked in the build inventory (§11): about 110 distinct labs across 25 patterns, plus 10 engines and 3 long-form patterns |\n| Studio briefs served makers only | Briefers had no Mastery path in the areas | Every studio brief has a Make variant (ANC) and a Commission variant (CMB); the same site pack, a different task |\n| Each area's medium-specific rules were implicit | The original's Practice DNA idea was lost | A Practice DNA unit at the start of every area's Practice tier |\n",
"| Peer features assumed a cohort | Self-paced learners would hit empty overlays | Pooled, anonymised outputs from all learners feed the overlays; the partner's Stranger voice stands in when the pool is thin (§9) |\n| The commerce, margin and expansion content was gone | The venture track could not answer \"where does the money come in\" | Five units restored (gap items 6–9) |\n| Tools carried no data-rich modelling | BIM and PLM, the bridge to real handover, were missing | T4.2a |\n| Voices were named inconsistently (Historian, Fabricator, CFO appeared without definition) | The partner had no spec | Seven voices defined once (§9) |\n| Hours lived in v2, not in the register | Slices could not be sized | Hours by depth per module (§6) |\n| Assets that cannot be free (regional readers, colour study) had no home | They were silently dropped | Program-produced assets list with what is done now and what waits for the regional vertical (§10) |\n\n**Not changed, deliberately.** The number of levels; the 21 + 10 module set; the six areas; the artifact schema; the regional slot as the last unit of every module; the no-grades rule; global first, region later.\n\n## Benchmarks\n\n",
"The closest institutional analogue is Central Saint Martins' MA Narrative Environments; the closest delivery analogue is Brilliant's lab-first, tutor-guided interactive model; v3.0 borrows structure from the first and mechanics from the second, and takes one thing each from four others.\n\n| Benchmark | What it does | What v3.0 adopts |\n|---|---|---|\n",
"| CSM MA Narrative Environments | <cite index=\"2-1\">Starts by charting contemporary and historical narrative environments, then critically demythologising, deconstructing, decolonising and decommodifying dominant narratives; systems observation and mapping is developed through counterfactuals and speculative histories to ground future propositions</cite>. <cite index=\"4-1\">Its modules run from methodology and scope, through challenging, originating and repositioning, to a major project proposal, the major project and a critical report</cite>, and it <cite index=\"2-1\">teaches budgets, project management, IP, collaboration, negotiation and proposal composition</cite>. It also maintains a networked course compendium of terms | The Foundations-to-Method order (chart, deconstruct, map, propose); a critical report as the written thread's capstone; the venture content as part of the design education, not an add-on; the glossary as a networked compendium rather than a list |\n",
"| Studio master's programs generally (RCA, DAE, the Bartlett) | Program specification and module descriptors with aims, outcomes, assessment and hours; external examination; the major project as the spine | Module descriptors (§6), the artifact as the major project, the defence as the examination |\n| Brilliant | <cite index=\"13-1\">Does not teach how to do something before asking questions; it lets learners try to find a solution before learning the procedure, and every lesson has practice sets</cite>. <cite index=\"11-1\">A digital tutor sees the lesson and the problem a learner is working on and guides the reasoning step by step instead of revealing the answer</cite>; <cite index=\"11-1\">courses are organised into learning paths with a suggested sequence</cite> | Lab before concept in every unit; the partner that questions rather than answers; routes as learning paths; practice sets as the module's closing test |\n| World Building Institute and game-design pedagogy | The world as the deliverable; bibles that other departments build from; rules that generate play | The Concept DNA as a bible; the Stranger test as the proof that others can build from it |\n",
"| Cohort schools (IDEO U, Hyper Island-type) | Live critique and peer pods as the product | Deferred: optional paid crit cohorts once there are learners; peer pods as a later feature |\n| AI tutoring products (Khanmigo-type) | Socratic tutoring inside the content, never grading | The partner's rules: questions, tests, no verdicts |\n\n**What no benchmark offers.** A single method spanning space, object, body, art, identity and digital product; two lanes on one spine; full studio depth in a self-paced format; a global-first world set with a regional vertical. That combination is the program's position.\n\n## The twelve principles of v3.0\n\nEverything in the program follows from twelve rules; if a future change breaks one of them, the change is wrong or the rule needs a versioned revision.\n\n1. **One curriculum, three depths.** Every module is written once at the original's depth. Orientation is its first fifteen minutes, Practice its usable core, Mastery its studio and defence. The Introduction, Practitioner and Comprehensive versions are slices by depth, never separate texts.\n",
"2. **Two lanes, one spine.** Makers produce; briefers write and judge. Same concepts, cases and labs; different Apply step and artifact attachments. Anyone may take any depth of any module. Every studio brief has a Make and a Commission variant.\n3. **Four named routes.** Maker, Briefer, Founder, Student: published default paths through the depths, changing nothing structurally. A learner without a project takes one of three seed worlds.\n4. **The artifact is the through-line.** The Concept DNA (schema in Master Structure §2) grows at the end of every unit, is tested at the end of every module, and is defended at the end of the program. Makers add anchor, family, adjacent translation and dossier; briefers add brief, judged set and commissioning record.\n",
"5. **The unit's shape follows its knowledge****.** There is no fixed sequence. Each unit opens with the move that is most impactful for its particular knowledge and research (the opening moves below), and orders its parts to suit. Four things are invariant: every unit contains a lab or simulation, an Apply step by lane, a partner step, and a log entry; and a term is named after the learner has met it, wherever that is possible. Every kept term enters the glossary, which is a networked compendium.\n6. **Tests, not grades.** Every module closes with a test (§6); the partner works through five lenses (generativity, craft, feasibility, cultural authority, evidence) and never issues a verdict.\n7. **Seven voices.** The partner's voices (§9) replace the studio's critic, fabricator, client, finance director, historian, planet and stranger. They ask, test and reflect; they do not grade.\n8. **Six threads.** Planetary Weight, Handover Grammar, Attribution and Authority, Accessibility, Writing and Argument, AI Collaboration run across all levels with checkpoints (§8), so recurring concerns progress instead of repeating.\n",
"9. **Precise vocabulary, no adjectives.** The profession's terms stay (typology, tectonics, affordance, palimpsest, tech-pack, MVI); manifesto intensifiers go; obscure headings are renamed (the v2 lists). The original's voice lives in provocations and studio briefs only.\n10. **Free and open, with a record.** Every asset is sourced from open collections, public-domain texts, open data or free software, or produced by the program; every asset carries licence, source and the units that use it.\n11. **Global spine, regional slot.** Every module's last unit is a regional slot; the world sets are global across the whole timeline; the Gulf, MENA and SWANA vertical fills the slots when it is built.\n12. **Evolving by version.** Research is uncapped and logged; findings change the structure through recorded versions (Master Structure §12); new units take letter suffixes so references never break.\n\n**Opening moves** (chosen per unit by the module designer, recorded in the unit spec)\n\n| Move | Opens with | Best when the knowledge is | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| Lab first | The interactive, no explanation | Perceptual, or a definition the learner can discover | F3.2 colour interaction; F2.1 archetype DNA; M2.2 the four tests |\n| Object first | One object or case, told as a story | Historical, cultural, material | F1 centres; F4.1 material as text; S2.12 emotional durability |\n| Story first | A scrollytelling narrative | Sequenced history, lineage, a transformation over time | F1.1 nine pivots; S3.11 silhouette and power; S1.14 palimpsest |\n| Problem first | A brief, a constraint or a failure to fix | Procedural, technical, economic | S1.27 egress; S2.34 value engineering; R3.6 MVI |\n| Prediction first | A question the learner answers before anything is shown | Warnings, counter-intuitive facts, ethics | F1.2 the spectrum; F5.8 what design cannot fix; T5.1 what a render claims |\n| Argument first | The claim and its counter-claim | Theory that the learner already half-holds | S1.9 phenomenology; S4.12 relational theory; S2.10 critical design |\n| Artifact first | The learner's own DNA or project, with a gap to fill | Method units late in a module; any Mastery unit | M2.17 full grammar authoring; M4.9 the anchor; R5.7 transfer brief |\n",
"| Partner first | A voice asks the question of the learner's work | Judgement and revision | M1.8 the insight; M4.7 abandoned directions; R5.3 defence |\n| Data first | A dataset or a map to explore | Systems, climate, markets, routes | F5.3 feedback; F1.11 trade routes; R2.4b expansion |\n| Making first | Hands on paper, card or the body before any screen | Tools, form, the body | T1.1 drawing; S2.19 the buck; S3.3 ease |\n\nThe research log of each module says which move each unit takes and why; when a unit's research shows a different move would land harder, the unit changes, and the change is versioned.\n\n**Sequencing rules.** Foundations before or alongside Method; Method before any area's Practice tier; an area's Mastery studio after M2 and M4 at Practice; tools attach to the unit that first needs them; venture modules after at least one area at Practice; R5 last. Experienced makers may open with M2 at Orientation as a preview.\n\n",
"**Frameworks and threshold concepts** (from the analysis, retained): backward design and constructive alignment; studio as signature pedagogy translated to the partner; threshold concepts taught slowly — history is a web, archetypes have DNA, the body is the metric, materials and words are signs, a concept is a generative system, generativity is testable, the collection precedes the object, the invisible systems are design material, circularity is the brief; cognitive apprenticeship (worked exemplar, then the learner's own); Kolb's cycle in the unit anatomy; mastery with re-submission instead of marks.\n\n## The program at module level\n\nTwenty-one modules and ten tools modules; hours are Orientation / Practice / Mastery for a learner, including the additions in §7; unit counts are v3.0.\n\n### Foundations\n\n| Module | Units | Hours O/P/M | Prerequisites | Closing test — maker | Closing test — briefer | Engine |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| O1 How to see | 4 | 2 / — / — | none | Read-a-Room annotation | same | — |\n",
"| F1 Histories of making | 23 | 4 / 14 / 16 | none | Object Autopsy on an unseen object, with attribution | Sort a case set on the reference-to-appropriation spectrum with reasons | Timeline Atlas |\n| F2 Form | 15 | 4 / 10 / 12 | none | Dismantle an archetype, keep its identity | Judge five subversions: which keep identity, which break it | Typology Dismantler |\n| F3 Body and senses | 13 | 4 / 10 / 10 | F2 | Sensory rules for a scene in the Sense Engine | Audit a supplied scene against the body and colour rules | Sense Engine |\n| F4 Material and meaning | 14 | 4 / 10 / 10 | F2 | Material and verbal grammar candidates with reasons | Judge a set of material and copy choices against a stated meaning | — |\n| F5 Systems and consequences | 12 | 3 / 8 / 10 | F4 | Ecosystem map and one scenario | Same, plus what the intervention cannot fix | Planetary Ledger |\n\n### Method\n\n| Module | Units | Hours O/P/M | Prerequisites | Closing test — maker | Closing test — briefer | Engine |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 Research and premise | 17 | 3 / 11 / 12 | Foundations at O | A premise defended against the Client voice | Same, on a premise written as a brief | — |\n",
"| M2 Concept DNA | 20 | 4 / 14 / 16 | M1 | The Stranger test: drift scored, one rule fixed, rerun | The Stranger builds from the brief; the briefer judges with the four tests | Stranger Lab |\n| M3 World architecture | 10 | 3 / 10 / 12 | M2 | Cutting Room: subtract a third of the collection with reasons | Judge a supplied collection's roles and coherence | Collection Matrix |\n| M4 Make and test | 12 | 2 / 10 / 30 | M3 | Adjacent translation with drift under threshold | Commission the partner as designer; judge three directions; revise the brief once | area engine |\n\n### Practice areas\n\n| Area | Units | Hours O/P/M | Prerequisites | Closing test — maker | Closing test — briefer | Engine |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 Space and experience | 38 | 2 / 17 / 72 | M2 (M4 for Mastery) | Anchor on the site pack; handover audited | Commission variant of the brief; judged set of five spaces | World Engine |\n| S2 Object and product | 41 | 2 / 16 / 60 | same | Object family from buck to BOM | Commission variant; judged set of five objects | Object Line |\n| S3 Body and wearables | 48 | 2 / 16 / 72 | same | Capsule with tech-pack | Commission variant; judged set of five garments | Capsule |\n",
"| S4 Art and installation | 38 | 2 / 16 / 50 | same | Site proposal with documentation plan | Commission variant; judged set of five works | Site Work |\n| S5 Identity and communication | 25 | 2 / 16 / 50 | same | Identity system a stranger can apply | Commission variant; judged set of five identities | Identity Engine |\n| S6 Digital product and interface | 21 | 2 / 16 / 50 | same | Prototype with tokens and accessibility audit | Commission variant; judged set of five flows | Product Loop |\n\n### Tools\n\n| Module | Units | Hours P/M | Attached to | Closing test |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 Seeing and drawing | 5 | 8 / 12 | F2, S1.7 | An orthographic set read by the Handover Auditor |\n| T2 Parametric thinking | 5 | 6 / 16 | F2.13, M3 | One rule regenerating a coherent family |\n| T3 Working with AI | 5 | 8 / 12 | M2.15 | A brief that reproduces the DNA under blind interpretation |\n| T4 Simulation, interface, data-rich models | 6 | 8 / 18 | F3.8, S1.31 | A walkthrough or prototype of the studio project |\n| T5 Honest evidence and documentation | 5 | 6 / 12 | M4, S4.7 | An evidence set with a representation-ethics statement |\n",
"| T6 Reality capture | 3 | — / 14 | S1.5, S2.19 | A clean capture used in an engine |\n| T7 Data visualisation | 4 | — / 14 | M1, F5 | An interactive view of the learner's data |\n| T8 Physical computing | 4 | — / 14 | S2.30, S3.25 | A sensing and acting behaviour, simulated or built |\n| T9 Environmental analytics | 2 | — / 14 | F5.6, S1.19 | A climate simulation and an LCA of the studio project |\n| T10 Fabrication | 3 | — / 12 | S1.17, S2.20 | A part with its toolpath and failure analysis |\n\n### Venture and release\n\n| Module | Units | Hours O/P/M | Prerequisites | Closing test — maker | Closing test — briefer | Engine |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 The world as venture | 16 | 2 / 11 / 12 | one area at P | Orbital architecture and carrier strategy defended | Judge a brand architecture for drift from its DNA | Venture Console |\n| R2 Money, law and rights | 13 | 2 / 11 / 12 | R1 | Two funding scenarios run; IP route chosen with reasons | Same, from the commissioner's side | Venture Console |\n",
"| R3 Making it real | 13 | 2 / 10 / 12 | one area at M for Mastery | Handover package audited; MVI negotiated with constants intact | The brief as artifact, audited; negotiation from the client side | Handover Auditor |\n| R4 Responsibility | 9 | 2 / 8 / 8 | R2 | Final planetary ledger with data | Judge a release plan's lifecycle claims | Planetary Ledger |\n| R5 Defence and dossier | 9 | 2 / 8 / 20 | everything | Transfer brief executed; defence in the Defence Room; dossier | Transfer brief as commission; defence of the judged sets; dossier | Defence Room |\n\n**Totals, v3.0.** About 450 units. Introduction slice about 48 h; Practitioner slice with one area about 215 h; Comprehensive with one studio and two tool electives about 500 h.\n\n## Unit additions and changes register, v3.0\n\nTwenty-two units are added and two renamed; the Master Structure remains the single unit register (so two copies cannot drift), and these rows are appended there under a v3.0 entry. Columns match the register.\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media | Follows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| F1.3a | Interdisciplinary discourse: what film, performance, sound, gaming and digital culture teach design | P | SRC, GRM.n | L, C | Cross-Discipline Case Wall · vid, aud, img | F1.3 |\n| F1.20a | Vernacular intelligence: oral history, folktale and local taxonomy as sources, with consent | M | SRC | L, K, D | Oral History Kit (consent, prompts, transcription) · aud | F1.20 |\n| F4.7 (renamed) | Semiotics, symbolism and collective memory | P | GRM.n | L, C | Sign-Swap · img | — |\n| F5.5a | Land, water and climate as the site | P | PER, PLW | L, C, I | Site Systems Map · dat | F5.5 |\n| M1.11a | World-building lessons from film, games and performance: bibles, rules, stakes, time | P | GRM.n, GRM.t | L, C | Bible Anatomy (dissect a public world bible) · vid | M1.11 |\n| S1.1a | The practice DNA of space: what the medium adds and forbids | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | S1.1 |\n| S1.8a | Exhibition, museum and cultural-district experience: object, story, visitor | P | GRM.s, GRM.n | C, I | Visitor Journey Mapper · img, drw | S1.8 |\n| S1.8b | The service script behind the space: hospitality, food and drink, operations | P | GRM.b, HND | C, I | Service Blueprint Builder | S1.8a |\n",
"| S1.24a | The outdoor room: landscape, shade, water, ground as spatial practice | M | GRM.s, GRM.a | C, S | World Engine, outdoor layer · dat | S1.24 |\n| S2.1a | The practice DNA of the object | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | S2.1 |\n| S3.1a | The practice DNA of the garment | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | S3.1 |\n| S3.35a | Accessories and jewellery: the object on the body | M | GRM.f, GRM.k | C, K, I | Fit Tester, body mode · img (open collections) | S3.35 |\n| S4.1a | The practice DNA of the installation | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | S4.1 |\n| S5.1a | The practice DNA of identity | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | S5.1 |\n| S6.1a | The practice DNA of the interface | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | S6.1 |\n| T4.2a | Data-rich models: BIM and PLM, cost and tolerance inside the geometry | M | HND | L, I | Data-Rich Model Explorer · 3d | T4.2 |\n| T5.1a | Documenting physical work: photography, capture, sequencing | P | DOS | L, K, I | Documentation Planner · img | T5.1 |\n| R1.10a | The physics of the checkout: navigation, friction, payment | P | VEN | C, I | Checkout Flow Dissector | R1.10 |\n",
"| R1.10b | Online-to-offline synchronisation | P | VEN | I | O2O Journey Mapper | R1.10a |\n| R1.10c | The flagship as media | P | VEN | C, I | Media-Value Calculator · img | R1.10b |\n| R1.12a | Large-scale activation: expos, festivals, civic events | M | VEN | C, S | Activation Workflow, scale mode · img | R1.12 |\n| R2.4a | Cost, value and margin architecture: from cost of goods to price | P | RGT | L, S | Venture Console, margin layer | R2.4 |\n| R2.4b | Expansion across markets: regulation, behaviour, logistics | P | RGT | L, I | Expansion Canvas | R2.4a |\n| R2.6 (renamed) | Contracts, NDAs, licensing, transfer of rights | P | RGT | I | Contract Clause Explorer | — |\n| R3.6a | Client and stakeholder negotiation | P | HND, CMB | S, P | Negotiation Simulator; Client voice | R3.6 |\n| R4.4a | Lifecycle economics: total cost of ownership | P | LCY, RGT | S | Venture Console, lifecycle layer | R4.4 |\n\n",
"**Rules changes that touch existing units.** Every Sx studio brief unit gains a Commission variant (CMB). T4 is retitled Simulation, interface and data-rich models; T5 is retitled Honest evidence and documentation. S1.21 gains scent and culinary case dossiers. F1.18 and R2.8 are the same protocol at two depths and say so.\n\n## The six threads\n\nA thread is a concern that recurs across levels and must progress rather than repeat; each has a named checkpoint per level and one owner engine or tool.\n\n| Thread | Level 1 | Level 2 | Level 3 | Tools | Level 4 | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Planetary Weight | F5.6 rough ledger; F5.10 first ledger | M3.6 cost and risk distribution includes ecological weight | Sx circularity units; the engine's carbon layer | T9.2 full LCA | R4.6–R4.7 final ledger; R4.4a lifecycle economics | Planetary Ledger |\n| 2. Handover Grammar | F2 orthographic language via T1 | M4.4 construction logic; M4.8 specialist gaps | S1.32, S2.34, S3.40, S6.18 area packs | T4.2a data-rich models | R3.8, R3.11 the audited package | Handover Auditor |\n",
"| 3. Attribution and Authority | F1.2 spectrum; F1.16 labour; F1.18 protocol; F1.20a vernacular sources | M1.5 ethics and consent; M1.14 fieldwork | S2.11 craft translation; S3.15, S3.46 regional dress; S4.13 mythologies | T3.5 bias and sovereignty | R2.7–R2.8 protocol and responses | Historian voice |\n| 4. Accessibility | F3.3 the body as metric; F3.4 cognitive load | M2.13 category obligations include access | S1.27 egress; S3.30 adaptive; S5.16 contrast; S6.6, S6.16 audits; a checkpoint in every studio brief | T5 evidence readable by all | R3.9 compliance | Accessibility Checker |\n| 5. Writing and Argument | One argument card answered per Foundations module | M1.8 the insight; M1.16 the defended premise | Each studio brief's statement of intent | T5.1a documentation | R3.3 pitch; R5.3 critical defence; the critical report in the dossier | Defence Room |\n| 6. AI Collaboration | F2.13 parametric first rule | M2.15, M2.18–19 Stranger and brief architecture | Sx.21 or equivalent: guidelines a stranger can apply; engines' generative layers | T3 all | R3.2 studio OS; R5.7 transfer brief | Stranger Lab |\n\n",
"**How a thread is used.** The app shows each thread as a progress line across the learner's route; a module cannot close while its thread checkpoint is empty; the dossier lists the six threads as six short chapters.\n\n## The AI partner\n\nSeven voices, one rule: they ask, test and reflect, and never say good or bad; between them they stand in for the critic, the fabricator, the client, the finance director, the historian, the planet and the stranger a studio would otherwise need in the room.\n\n| Voice | Asks | Where it appears | What it can see |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The Mirror | \"Here is what your work is doing; is that what you meant?\" | Every Apply step; M1.8; M4.7 | The learner's artifact and the unit's concept |\n| The Stranger | \"I have never met you; here is what I built from your rules.\" | M2.15, M2.18; every Sx.21-type unit; R5.7 | The DNA or brief only, never the conversation |\n| The Client | \"I am commissioning this; what do I get, what does it cost, what could go wrong?\" | Briefs; M1.16; R3.3; R3.6a; S4.8 | The DNA, the brief, the venture fields |\n",
"| The Fabricator | \"This will not build; here is why, and what it would take.\" | M4.8; every studio brief; R3.6; T10 | The engine state, materials, tolerances |\n| The CFO | \"This costs that; cut this and you break these constants.\" | S1.29, S2.34, S3.32; R2; R3.12 | The Venture Console and the DNA constants |\n| The Historian | \"Where does this come from, who made it, whom did you ask?\" | F1; M1.14; S2.11; S3.15; R2.7 | The world set, the sources, the attribution record |\n| The Planet | \"What does this weigh, and where does it go when it dies?\" | F5; Sx circularity; R4 | The Planetary Ledger and the BOM |\n\n**Rules.** No verdicts; every response ends with a question or a test the learner can run. The five lenses (generativity, craft, feasibility, cultural authority, evidence) shape what each voice looks at, not a score. Voices declare their agenda. Everything generated is logged, attributable and scoreable for drift. Free-tier limits are shown, not hidden.\n\n",
"**Standing in for peers.** Overlays (the cohort's sorts, thresholds, ratings) draw on a pooled, anonymised set of all learners' outputs; when the pool is thin, the Stranger produces the comparison set and says so. Peer exchange in M2.18 is the Stranger until real exchange is available; live crit cohorts are a later, optional product.\n\n**Standing in for specialists.** The Fabricator, CFO and Historian carry the specialist consultations the original studios assumed; each has a knowledge pack per area (materials and processes, cost norms, source lists) that the research logs feed.\n\n## Research, resources, media and lecture rules\n\nResearch is uncapped and typed; sources are free and recorded; media is mostly sourced and sometimes made; lecture material is whatever must be told, kept to a fifth of the time and always preceded by a lab.\n\n**Research plan by module type**\n\n| Type | Modules | What it needs | How it is done | What you check |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| Historical and cultural | F1, F4, M1.11a, Sx lineage units, S5.11, S6.9 | World sets of 40–60 objects and cases with open images, dates, provenance, makers; scholarship; public-domain primary sources | Scans of open-access scholarship through the academic connectors; object selection from CC0 collection APIs; object stories written from the record and cited | Balance across regions and eras; fairness; five facts per module spot-checked |\n| Perceptual and skills | F2, F3, T1–T5 | Stable principles; good examples; the labs | Principles cross-checked once; examples from open collections or the learner's own; labs built and tested | Whether the lab produces the perception it claims; wording for non-designers |\n| Method | M1–M4 | Exemplar grammars and collection logic; the program's worked world | Public brand and design-system guidelines, game bibles and pattern languages read for structure; the exemplar DNA written and tested in the Stranger Lab | Taste and coherence of the exemplar |\n",
"| Practice | S1–S6, T6–T10 | Global case sets; the medium's constraints; free-tool documentation; free standards | Case scans; open 3D and drawings; tutorials written against the tool's own docs; practitioner voices from public talks with attribution | Realism: what a practitioner would object to |\n| Venture | R1–R5 | Plain-language public guidance on money, IP, commerce, handover; open templates | WIPO and national IP guides; public finance and commerce guides; open contract and brief templates; every unit labelled literacy | That nothing reads as advice; formulas visible |\n\n**Research outputs per module** (always the same four files): source list with licences; case library entries (object, image link, licence, date, place, maker, story, units); claims sheet; open questions for you. Research logs and sprint entries live in the Master Structure §12.\n\n**Program-produced assets** (what the analysis called commissioned; now made by the program from open sources, in this order)\n\n| Asset | Serves | Now or later |\n|---|---|---|\n| The world set: about 1,200 objects and cases, tagged by unit, with a regional slot per module | all | Now, module by module |\n",
"| The exemplar Concept DNA and three seed worlds | M1–M4, S1–S6 | Now, before M2 |\n| The networked glossary (compendium), with \"say it to a designer\" lines | all | Now, grows per module |\n| Argument cards (claim, counter-claim, case) for every theory unit | F, M, Sx theory units | Now, per module |\n| Site, object, body and brand packs per area | Sx studio briefs | With each area |\n| Partner knowledge packs per area (materials, processes, cost norms, sources) | the Fabricator, CFO, Historian | With each area |\n| A global history reader in the program's own voice | F1 | Now, as F1 is built |\n| Regional readers: history, materials, colour and light, dress | regional slots | Later, with the regional vertical |\n\n",
"**Free sources and rules** (from Concept v1, unchanged): CC0 museum collections and their APIs for images; public-domain texts for primary sources; open-access scholarship; open data; CC 3D; Google Fonts; open-source and free-tier software with a named fallback for every free tier; embedding rather than re-hosting video; a licence note on every asset; paraphrase first, quotations short, no summaries that replace a copyrighted book; assets referenced by link where licence requires.\n\n**Media rules** (from Master Structure §10): a unit's media list is part of its spec; nothing is Drafted without media sourced or scheduled; every asset records licence, source and units; production is limited to drawings, animated diagrams, engine recordings, synthesised audio and the packs.\n\n",
"**Lecture rules** (from Master Structure §11): six kinds of lecture material (sequenced narrative, theory and argument, definitions and taxonomies, procedures, cases, warnings); each has a conversion (navigate before reading, argument card then lab, sort then reveal, the tool is the procedure, cases as dossiers, ethics as simulators); text first, video only where motion carries meaning, nothing over eight minutes; about a fifth of learner time.\n\n**Reference canon** (for our grounding, not for learners, since it is not free): the reading lists in the analysis §10 stand; the program's own copy replaces them for learners, with attribution.\n\n## Presentation forms for explanatory content\n\nAnything that must be explained gets a form chosen for the kind of explanation it is, from a catalogue of eighteen; layered, animated and scroll-driven pages are the default for narrative and theory, step-through explainers for procedures and systems, and plain text is the fallback, not the norm.\n\n| # | Form | What it is | Best for (lecture kind) | Example units | Free stack | Effort |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| 1 | Scrollytelling page | Multi-layered, parallax page: images, drawings or 3D as depth layers, text pinned and revealed as the reader scrolls; strata, time and scale become literal layers | Sequenced narrative; theory that has a spatial or temporal shape | F1.1 nine pivots; F1.11 routes; S3.11 silhouette history; S1.10 courtyard to tower; F4.6 patina; S1.14 palimpsest | CSS scroll-driven animations; a scroll library; three.js for 3D layers | M–L |\n| 2 | Step-through explainer | A diagram or scene that builds one step at a time; the learner advances, can go back, can branch | Procedures; systems; taxonomies | F5.3 feedback loops; M2.6 the twelve grammars; S1.27 egress; R3.8 handover anatomy; S2.26 tolerances | SVG plus a small state machine | S–M |\n| 3 | Object story | An annotated image with hotspots, deep zoom and toggled layers (material, labour, trade, meaning) | Cases | Every C unit; the Object Autopsy's reading mode | IIIF deep-zoom viewers (OpenSeadragon) on museum images | S |\n",
"| 4 | Explorable explanation | Prose with live variables the reader drags inside the sentence; the text recomputes | Definitions with thresholds; anything with a formula | M2.4 thresholds; F3.6 colour physics; R2.2 dilution; S1.29 yield | in-app components | S–M |\n| 5 | Before/after and morph | A slider or scrub between two states, or across time | Change over time; subversion; reuse | F2.13; F4.6; S1.5; S3.18 | image compare; morph targets | S |\n| 6 | Animated diagram | A short looping motion graphic with no narration, next to the text | Concept cards; mechanisms | F2.10 grids; F3.5 joints; S2.18 kinetic states | SVG or Lottie-type animation | S |\n| 7 | Short video | Under eight minutes; only where motion carries meaning; engine screen recordings; embedded public talks | Motion-dependent content; practitioner voices | S3.16 drape; S2.19 the buck; S4.20 light as mass | screen capture; embeds | S–M |\n| 8 | Audio companion | Narrated track to listen while doing; field-walk guides; argument cards read aloud | Making units; walks; revision | M1.14 field sprint; S1.9 phenomenology walk; any argument card | synthesised narration | S |\n",
"| 9 | 3D turntable and walkthrough embed | A model or site the reader orbits or walks, inside the page | Form, scale, space | F2.11 scale; S1.31; S2.16 | three.js viewer | M |\n| 10 | Comparison wall | Filterable grid of cases side by side, with a shared lens | Case sets; judging practice | Every module's judged set; S5.11; S6.9 | in-app grid | S |\n| 11 | Timeline scrub | The Timeline Atlas in reading mode: drag through time, layers on and off | Narrative history | F1; S3.11; S5.11 | Timeline Atlas | reuse |\n| 12 | Interactive map | Places, routes and flows | Trade routes; sites; expansion | F1.11; S1.24; R2.4b | map library on OSM | M |\n| 13 | Argument card | Claim, counter-claim, one case, expandable to the essay | Theory | S1.9; S4.12; F2.4; S2.10 | in-app card | S |\n| 14 | Sequential illustration | Comic-strip panels for rituals, use choreography, service scripts, audience journeys | Procedures with people in them | S2.4; S1.8b; S4.30; M1.11 | drawn panels, in-app | M |\n| 15 | Prediction prompt | A question the reader answers before the reveal, inline in the text | Warnings; definitions; any claim worth testing | throughout | Guess-and-Reveal pattern | reuse |\n",
"| 16 | Guided sketch-along | Draw with the page: overlays and steps on the learner's own canvas | Drawing and notation | T1; F2.1; S1.7 | Drawing Trainer | reuse |\n| 17 | The designed brief | A studio brief laid out as a document with the program's voice, printable | Studio briefs | every Sx studio brief | in-app, exportable | S |\n| 18 | Two-voice dialogue | Historian and Client (or Fabricator and CFO) argue the unit's question, generated and optional | Theory and warnings for listeners | argument units; R2; R3 | partner voices, synthesised audio | S |\n\n",
"**Rules for the forms.** Choose by lecture kind, not by taste: narrative → 1, 11, 3; theory → 13, 1, 18; definitions and taxonomies → 2, 4, 15; procedures → 2, 14, 16; cases → 3, 10; warnings → 15, 14, 18. Parallax and depth layers are used only where the layers mean something (strata, time, scale, inside and outside), never as decoration. Every animated form has a reduced-motion version and a text alternative; every page reads on a phone; nothing autoplays with sound. One form per lecture element, two at most; the unit's opening move (§5) decides whether the form or the lab comes first. Every form is a component built once and filled with content, so the catalogue is part of the build inventory (§11).\n\n## Consumption matrix\n\nWhat a learner needs to learn comes in five kinds, and how they consume it comes in six situations; every unit is published in three consumption forms so that the same content meets the learner on a phone for five minutes, at a desk for an hour, and in the hand while making.\n\n**What they need to learn** (by level and lane)\n\n| Kind of knowledge | What it is | Heaviest in | Maker needs | Briefer needs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| Know-that | Facts, histories, names, dates, sources | Foundations, lineage units | Enough to place their own work | Enough to talk to designers and check claims |\n| Know-why | Theory, argument, judgement | Foundations, Method, venture | To defend choices | To judge choices |\n| Know-how | Procedures, moves, tool skills | Method, areas, tools | The full procedure | The procedure's shape, to brief and audit it |\n| Know-who and know-where | Sources, makers, communities, institutions, archives | Attribution thread, regional slots | To credit and consult | To commission and credit |\n| Dispositions | Habits: test before trusting, subtract, log changes, ask who made it | Everywhere; the partner's questions | Both | Both |\n\n**How and where they consume** (situations, with what fits each)\n\n| Situation | Time | Attention | What fits | Forms | Depth |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Phone, in transit | 5–10 min | Fragmented, one hand | A provocation, one lab, one card, one prediction prompt | 15, 13, 3, 6, 11 in scrub mode | Orientation |\n| Desk, focused | 45–90 min | Full | The whole unit: lab, concept, cases, apply, partner | 1, 2, 4, 9, 10, the labs | Practice |\n",
"| Hands busy, making | 30–120 min | Ears free, eyes on the work | Audio companion, step-through on a second screen, the designed brief printed | 8, 2, 17, 16 | Practice, Mastery |\n| Walking or on site | 20–60 min | Outdoors, moving | Audio walk, field prompts, capture tasks | 8, 12, capture tools | Mastery (M1.14, S1.9, S4.26) |\n| Reviewing before a decision | 10–20 min | Skimming, comparing | Comparison wall, glossary compendium, the artifact's own fields, the judged set | 10, 13, the DNA Builder read mode | Any |\n| Revisiting as reference | 2–5 min | Look-up | Glossary entry, card, checklist, formula panel | 13, 4, checklists | Any |\n\n**Three published forms per unit.** The five-minute form (Orientation layer: the unit's opening move, one lab or card); the session form (the full unit anatomy); the reference form (glossary entries, cards, checklists and the artifact fields the unit filled). The app serves the form by device and session length; a learner can switch. Studio briefs also exist as printable documents; making units have an audio companion; field units have an offline pack.\n\n",
"**Modes of consumption a unit should offer** (at least three of six): read, watch, listen, play, make, browse. Foundations units lean read-play-browse; Method units play-make-read; studio units make-play-listen; venture units play-read-browse.\n\n**By career stage and lane** (defaults, not limits): a student reads and plays most; a mid-career designer makes and skims theory; a founder browses, plays the simulators and listens; a briefer reads cards, browses walls and plays the judging labs. Routes (§5) set these defaults; the three published forms make them possible without duplicating content.\n\n**Design consequences.** Every unit's spec now carries its consumption forms and its presentation forms alongside its media list; the glossary is built as reference-first (look-up in two taps); the Orientation layer is written to be complete on a phone; audio is generated for every argument card and every making unit; and the pattern library gains the presentation catalogue as a second component set.\n\n## Build inventory, pipeline, rhythm and alternatives\n\n",
"The program is built from 25 reusable patterns, 3 long-form patterns and 10 engines, instantiated as about 110 distinct labs; the pipeline runs through this account's tools with a human at the two points that need one, and the build order proves the spine before the studios.\n\n**Build inventory**\n\n| Kind | Count | Examples | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reusable patterns (analysis §8) | 25 | Structured Editor (DNA Builder), Generative Lab, Spectrum Sort, Guess-and-Reveal, Calculator, Systems Map, Matrix Planner, Revision History, Parametric Morph, 3D Sandbox, Sequence Timeline, Role-Play, Synthesis Board, Card Deck, Decision Tree, Bilingual Text, Layered Timeline, Layer and Time, Scenario Generator, Journey Mapper, Routing Puzzle, Node Playground, Drawing Trainer, Simplified Physics, Unfolder | Eight priority-1 patterns ship the spine |\n| Long-form patterns | 3 | Studio Workspace (project, site pack, prototypes, revision history, partner threads in one place); Handover Auditor; Defence Room | With the first studio |\n",
"| Compound engines (Master Structure §9) | 10 | Timeline Atlas, Sense Engine, Stranger Lab, World Engine, Object Line, Capsule, Site Work, Identity Engine, Product Loop, Venture Console | Stranger Lab, Sense Engine, Timeline Atlas first |\n| Distinct labs | about 110 | pattern instances with content; reuse marked in the register | Per module |\n| Partner voices | 7 | with knowledge packs per area | Mirror, Stranger, Client first |\n\n**Pipeline** (from Concept v1, retained): concept and curriculum in Claude Docs; research through web, Exa, Firecrawl, Elicit, Scite, Consensus, Scholar Gateway, LlamaParse, Hugging Face; program voice through the brand-voice skills; visual design in Figma and the artifact Design type; labs as artifacts with the web-artifacts, frontend-design and algorithmic-art skills; the app on Lovable and Supabase (Base44 as fallback); learner testing with the user-research skills; assets in Drive or Dropbox; operations by scheduled tasks and the Module Factory skill; quality through accessibility, validation and code-review skills. Human points: taste and fairness; the last look at facts and rights.\n\n",
"**Roles I play; what stays with you.** I draft, research, build and revise; you decide, judge, spot-check, recruit the five testers and set the voice. Regional partners, guest critics and institutional relationships are listed in the analysis §10 for the day they are wanted.\n\n**Things at hand instead of kits.** Paper, card, a phone camera and the learner's own body, kitchen and wardrobe cover Foundations, Method and the Practice tier of every area; Mastery studios list an optional kit (foam, callipers, a half-scale form, a microcontroller) with a no-kit route through the engines.\n\n**Working rhythm** (2–3 days a week): a weekly digest of what moved; one module in the factory at a time; research sprints logged; structure versions recorded; your review day closes each module's stage.\n\n**Build order.** Fix the DNA schema and pack format → Practice tier of Foundations and Method → Mastery of M2 and M4 → Practice tier of all six areas → Mastery studio for S2 → Practice tier of venture → Mastery of R3 and R5 → remaining studios and tool electives through the factory → the regional vertical.\n\n",
"**Alternatives considered** (Concept v2 §6, retained for the record): two products; core plus pathways; studio-first; region-first. The chosen shape is depth dials with named routes. **Nine angles** for reading the program (Concept v2 §5) remain a checklist for every future version: lane, depth, artifact, medium, knowledge type, traditional versus innovative, geography, career stage, time budget. **Future area S7, synthetic worlds as a medium**, is noted for when S5 and S6 exist.\n\n## Change log and next steps\n\nv3.0 is the working baseline: this document holds the framework, the Master Structure holds the unit register with the v3.0 additions appended, and every later change is a versioned entry in both.\n\n**Change log**\n\n- v3.0 — Audit of five versions; 38 items restored or resolved; 22 units added, 2 renamed; six threads, closing tests, prerequisites, seven partner voices, commissioning variants, reuse marking; hours by depth restated; benchmarks recorded. Unit count about 450.\n\n",
"- v3.1 — Added the presentation-forms catalogue (18 forms with rules) and the consumption matrix (five kinds of knowledge, six situations, three published forms per unit); unit specs now carry consumption and presentation forms; the build inventory gains the presentation catalogue as a component set.\n\n- v3.2 — Principle 5 revised: no fixed unit sequence; each unit opens with the move that suits its knowledge (ten opening moves catalogued), with four invariants (a lab or simulation, an Apply by lane, a partner step, a log). Presentation-form and consumption rules updated to match.\n\n**What is still open**\n\n1. Learner personas (one maker, one briefer, one founder, one student) in their own words.\n2. The program's name and the artifact's name.\n3. The exemplar world: invented, or something of yours.\n4. Confirmation of F1 as the first module through the factory, M2 second, S2 the first studio.\n5. The six decisions from Concept v2 §8 that remain provisional: depth dials with named routes (assumed yes); six areas (assumed yes, original four built first); Method before Foundations allowed (assumed yes); first studio S2 (assumed yes); the vocabulary lists (assumed agreed).\n\n",
"**Pass 2 opens when you confirm item 4.** Its first output is F1's research log, not a lesson.\n"
